  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThe northeast winds are developing southward early this \nmorning, as surface high pressure begins to build through the \nQuad State. However, the cooling and drying surge seems a bit\nslow to arrive. The NBM has trended back up with temperatures\nand heat indices today. Therefore, we have added a handful of\ncounties to today's Heat Advisory. It now roughly covers the\narea south of a Greenville Missouri to Murray Kentucky line.\nThis should be the end of the heat headlines for a few days, if\nnot a significantly longer period.\n\nThe less hot temperatures and low dewpoints in the upper 50s are\nstill expected across the entire region for Labor Day.\nUnfortunately, the upper ridge will begin to build back over \nthe Quad State Monday and south winds at the surface will return\non Tuesday. This will lead to another warming trend Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday. Temperatures will climb back into the middle\n90s Tuesday and the 95-100 degree range Wednesday. Dewpoints\nwill rebound into the 60s, but even on Wednesday heat indices\nwill only reach the 100-105 range, which would not require a\nHeat Advisory.\n\nThe ridge will give way to weak zonal flow allowing a cold front\nand with a decent chance of showers and thunderstorms to move\nthrough the Quad State at some point Wednesday night into \nThursday. The NBM currently has PoPs ranging from 30% in the\nsouth to 50% in the north. While there is QPF throughout the\nregion, it is generally a half inch or less, which is not\nexactly going to reverse our current dry spell. \n\nThe weak zonal flow aloft will continue Friday through next \nweekend. Temperatures will trend back to normal or just \nslightly above. The zonal flow aloft will seriously muddle the \nprecipitation chances and timing. For now, the NBM has small \nchances nearly everyday.",
